    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d321">

                <pb facs="0304" n="290" sameAs="frus:frus1942v06/pg290"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">837.51/2862: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Cuba</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Briggs</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Habana</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-03-03T18:00:00-05:00">March 3, 1942—6
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 8:16 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>98. For Walmsley.<anchor n="49" xml:id="d321-f1"/> The Minister of
                        Hacienda,<anchor n="50" xml:id="d321-f2"/> López Castro<anchor n="51"
                        xml:id="d321-f3"/> and Subsecretary of Defense, Perez Benitoa will leave
                    Wednesday morning by airplane for Miami probably [apparent omission] the Cuban
                    Embassy of the exact hour of arrival in Washington).</p>
                <p>The Minister of Hacienda wishes to take up the various financial matters under
                    study by our Treasury officials and also final conclusion of the 25 million
                    dollars credit operation López Castro participating in the latter negotiation.
                    According to the Minister of State<anchor n="52" xml:id="d321-f4"/> the Cuban
                    Government will seek certain changes in the current credit draft but Cortina
                    expressed the opinion that no changes in principal are contemplated and that he
                    hopes final agreement can be reached within a very few days.</p>
                <p>López Castro has been instructed urgently to supplement Concheso’s<anchor n="53"
                        xml:id="d321-f5"/> representation concerning shipping and will also desire
                    to discuss sugar.</p>
                <p>The Subsecretary of Defense has been directed by the President to endeavor to
                    expedite delivery of at least some of the material now on order (please see my
                    telegram No. 74, February 21, 3 a.m.<anchor n="54" xml:id="d321-f6"/>).</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Briggs</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d321-f1" n="49" xml:id="d321-f1-content">
                <p>Walter N. Walmsley, Jr., Assistant Chief, Division of the American Republics.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d321-f2" n="50" xml:id="d321-f2-content">
                <p>Oscar García Montes.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d321-f3" n="51" xml:id="d321-f3-content">
                <p>Cuban Secretary of the Presidency.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d321-f4" n="52" xml:id="d321-f4-content">
                <p>José Manuel Cortina.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d321-f5" n="53" xml:id="d321-f5-content">
                <p>Aurelio F. Concheso, Cuban Ambassador in the United States.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d321-f6" n="54" xml:id="d321-f6-content">
                <p>Not printed; for correspondence on the problems of hemisphere defense, see <ref
                        target="#pg253">pp. 253</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
